<SECTION image-layout="blank" vertical-align="start">
  <DIV class="body">
    <H3>Sector Headwinds</H3>
    <COLUMNS colWidths="[33,33,34]">
      <DIV>
        <H4><B>Capital Intensity</B></H4>
        <P>Hardware-heavy climate solutions require $50-200M in project finance before revenue generation. Manufacturing scale demands gigafactory investments averaging $2.8B for battery plants, $1.2B for solar module facilities. Working capital needs stress venture-backed companies as production ramps exceed 18-month timelines.</P>
        <P><B>Financing Gaps</B> emerge in the $25-100M range where venture capital ends and project finance begins. Development capital markets remain underdeveloped for climate hardware. Asset-heavy business models struggle with traditional VC return expectations, creating systematic funding shortfalls for promising technologies approaching commercial scale.</P>
      </DIV>
      <DIV>
        <H4><B>Regulatory Risk</B></H4>
        <P>Policy uncertainty creates deployment friction across all climate sectors. IRA tax credit transferability rules remain unclear 18 months post-passage. Permitting timelines average 4.2 years for transmission projects, 2.8 years for utility-scale renewables. State-level policy reversals in Texas and Florida disrupted $8B in planned investments during 2024.</P>
        <P><B>Compliance Complexity</B> increases with overlapping federal, state, and local requirements. Environmental review processes lack standardization across jurisdictions. Grid interconnection rules vary by ISO, creating developer confusion. Carbon accounting standards remain fragmented, complicating corporate procurement decisions and limiting market liquidity.</P>
      </DIV>
      <DIV>
        <H4><B>Technical Barriers</B></H4>
        <P>Materials science limitations in carbon capture constrain cost reduction below $150/ton CO2. Sorbent degradation requires replacement every 18-24 months. High-temperature industrial processes lack viable electrification pathways. Hydrogen storage density remains 3x below gasoline energy equivalent, limiting transportation applications.</P>
        <P><B>Manufacturing Bottlenecks</B> persist in critical minerals processing. Lithium refining capacity trails demand by 340,000 tons annually. Rare earth element concentration in China creates supply chain vulnerabilities. Advanced materials like solid-state electrolytes require entirely new production infrastructure, delaying commercial deployment until 2028-2030.</P>
      </DIV>
    </COLUMNS>
  </DIV>
</SECTION>